  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 259 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will remain in place over the southern Rockies. \nThis will promote continued zonal to quasi-northwesterly flow aloft \nfor the Central Plains early in the week. Eventually, the upper \nridge will retrograde and allow predominant northwesterly flow to \nbecome established across the region. Multiple disturbances will \ndrop southeast within the mid-level flow and these along with \nassociated trailing cool front should promote near-daily rain and \nthunderstorm chances locally with temperatures favoring near to \nslightly above normal for late August. The expected proximity to a \nmid-level speed max will largely keep severe weather in play though \nday-to-day predictability will largely hinge on mesoscale features \nthat coarser mid-range models will struggle to resolve at this \ntimescale. While the pattern doesn't suggest widespread beneficial \nrainfall across the area, NBM probabilities suggest at least the \npotential for a few areas to see wetting rains next week. This is \nespecially true each afternoon/evening Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday \nwhere probabilities for exceeding 0.25\" rainfall climb to around 25-\n35 percent. As mentioned before, this will largely come down to \nsmaller scale features which won't be forecasted with greater \nconfidence for a few days still.",
